<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Round Orange.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts. Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to and name familiar food and shape-related words in simple texts (e.g., recognizing the word "apple" in the sentence, "The apple is round."). Can identify simple words related to shapes (e.g., pointing to a cracker and saying "square"). Can recognize sounds of letters related to the names of food items when supported with visuals (e.g., identifying the /ɔ/ sound in "orange" along with the letter O, or the /b/ sound in "banana" when shown its crescent shape).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a quick "Shape Chant" from Lesson 1, using flashcards of an orange (round), apple (round), and cracker (square). The teacher holds up each flashcard and says, "What is this? It's an orange! What shape is it? It's round!" Students repeat chorally, pointing to their heads for "round" or clapping hands for "square."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards with the words "round" and "orange" written clearly. The teacher points to the word "round" and says, "Round." Then points to "orange" and says, "Orange." The teacher then shows a simple sentence like "The orange is round." on a large card and slowly points to each word while reading it aloud, emphasizing the words "orange" and "round." The teacher also models the /ɔ/ sound in "orange" and the /r/ sound in "round."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a picture of a round orange) "Is this an orange? Yes or No?" (Students respond chorally or point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to the word 'round' on a flashcard) "Is this word 'square' or 'round'?" (Students point to 'round' or say 'round').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture chart titled "Shapes on Our Plates" with simple illustrated sentences. Each illustration shows a food item (e.g., orange, apple, cracker, banana) and below it, a short sentence describing its shape. Examples: "This is an orange. It is round." "This is an apple. It is round." "This is a cracker. It is square." "This is a banana. It is oval."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts; identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads each sentence aloud, pointing to the words. Students listen and point to the corresponding food picture. "Point to the round apple." "Point to the square cracker."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word Recognition:</b> The teacher calls out a word (e.g., "cracker"). Students find the word on the chart and point to it. The teacher then asks, "What shape is the cracker?" (Students point to 'square' on the chart).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sound-Letter Connection:</b> The teacher points to the word "orange" and asks, "What sound do you hear at the beginning?" (/ɔ/). Then points to the letter 'O'. The teacher repeats for 'banana' (/b/) and the letter 'B', and 'apple' (/æ/) and the letter 'A'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading familiar words, identifying shapes in text, phonemic awareness. Grammar: Adjective + noun ("round apple", "square cracker"), Present simple ("Oranges are round."). Vocabulary: orange, apple, cracker, banana, pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty. Pronunciation: /r/ in "round", /k/ in "cracker", /æ/ in "apple". Pragmatic: Identifying food shapes. Sociolinguistic: Describing food by shape and color.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-Picture Matching (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of food items (orange, apple, cracker, banana, pineapple, mango) and a word bank of corresponding words (orange, apple, cracker, banana, pineapple, mango) and shape words (round, square, oval). Students draw a line from each picture to its correct food word and then to its correct shape word. The teacher monitors and helps with pronunciation, specifically /r/ and /k/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Fill-in-the-Shape (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays a big picture of a "meal" with various foods (e.g., a "juice box," "banana," "cracker," "lemon," "lime," "papaya"). Below each item, there's a simple sentence with a missing shape word, e.g., "The cracker is ______." (Options: round, square, oval). Students are provided with shape word flashcards. The teacher reads each sentence, and students hold up the correct shape word flashcard to complete the sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Read My Plate" (Pair Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a "plate" template with 3-4 spaces. In each space, there's a picture of a food item (e.g., small apple, big banana, orange, cracker) and a simple descriptive sentence below it (e.g., "This is a small apple. It is round."). Student A points to a sentence and reads it aloud to Student B. Student B checks if Student A read it correctly by looking at the picture and the words. They then switch roles. Teacher circulates, listening for accurate reading and word recognition.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Healthy Food Display" Group Reading.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a large poster board (their "display table") and several cutout pictures of food items (e.g., pineapple, lemon, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, orange) along with pre-written simple labels (e.g., "This is a round orange.", "This is a square cracker."). The task is for each group to arrange their "healthy food" items on their "display table." Then, one student from each group is chosen to "present" their display by pointing to each food item and reading its label aloud to the class. Other group members help by pointing to the words or repeating the descriptions. This activity focuses on reading recognition and associating words with visuals in a meaningful context related to "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in recognizing familiar words, collaborative interaction in guiding peers to read, and communicative task completion by reading all labels on their "food display."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Healthy Food Display" and a quick individual check for word recognition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual "Point and Read" activity. The teacher shows students a flashcard with a simple sentence (e.g., "The lemon is round."). The student points to and attempts to read the words "lemon" and "round." Alternatively, the teacher shows a picture of a "mango" and asks students to find and point to the word "mango" from a set of two or three words. The teacher also asks, "What sound does 'mango' start with?" while pointing to the letter M.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to correctly point to familiar food words (e.g., "orange," "cracker," "apple") in simple texts/labels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to correctly point to shape words (e.g., "round," "square") when presented visually or auditorily.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of initial sounds of target food items (e.g., /ɔ/ for "orange," /b/ for "banana") with visual support.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a paper with a happy face and a sad face. They draw a circle around the happy face if "I can read many food words!" or the sad face if "I need more help reading words." They also draw a small food item they can read the word for.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you learn to read today about food or shapes?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you read 'The orange is round.'?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading activities worked best for 1st graders at Pre-A1.3 to recognize words and connect them to pictures? The "Read My Plate" activity was highly engaging and effective for word-picture association.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to reinforce reading skills and prepare for speaking? More small group guided reading with emphasis on specific tricky words. Perhaps incorporate a game where students have to read a word to earn a point.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided students with significant exposure and practice recognizing food and shape words in simple sentences. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use these recognized words to actively describe foods and their shapes in spoken interactions, building on their reading comprehension to produce oral language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>